`lib/WWW/PayPal/API/Webhooks.pm:130`: `$meta =~ s/\}\z/,"webhook_event":$raw_body}/;`. `raw_body` is the body of an unauthenticated inbound request; the only check is that it is defined, non-empty and not a reference.

A body that closes the value early adds sibling keys to the verification request, including a second `webhook_id`:

```
$pp->webhooks->verify( webhook_id => 'WH-MINE', raw_body => '{"id":"E"},"webhook_id":"WH-ATTACKER"', ... );
# sends {"webhook_id":"WH-MINE", ..., "webhook_event":{"id":"E"},"webhook_id":"WH-ATTACKER"}
```

`webhook_id` is the one field of that request the sender of the webhook is not supposed to control: it is what binds the signature to *our* endpoint. With last-key-wins parsing on PayPal's side, an event genuinely signed for the attacker's own webhook would verify as `SUCCESS`. Whether PayPal's parser is last-wins and whether it checks the webhook id against the calling app is not verified; and a consumer that afterwards `decode_json`s the same bytes strictly would die on the trailing garbage. So this is hardening, not a demonstrated bypass -- but it is one cheap check on the security-critical path.

Done when: `verify` returns 0 (not croak: a forged request must not become a 500) unless `raw_body` decodes as exactly one JSON object, the bytes sent stay the untouched original, and `t/webhooks.t` has the case above.

